 I like to  think think of things that are more like  us as being more evolved that's actually  the wrong way to think because whatever  advantages happen to be there in an  environment at a particular time  evolution just yeah it doesn't have any  sort of goal any sort of direction  whales were not exactly sure what  enticed them back into the ocean but the  evolution of whales seems to have  happened a few million years after the  extension of the dinosaurs and during  the time of the dinosaurs there were  also lots of other weird predators  they're like reptilian large reptilian  predators in the ocean they all died out  with the dinosaurs as well it's possible  it was just that just the fact that  there was this open niche or niche  depending how you want to say that it  was space there and so whales filled it  the earliest whales that we have in the  fossil record we can tell from isotopes  which is isotopes and their teeth we can  tell that they were most likely  freshwater so the early whales probably  lived somewhat like otters do today and  they were freshwater they were eating  plants they're eating fish and  eventually they were enticed to a full  aquatic lifestyle so just slowly but  surely they give up their you know land  traits and we just still see that they  have lungs and they have the remnants of  hind legs and then in the fossil record  we've coarse trace trace them back to  these land animals so evolution is a  meandering process kind of like my  response was next question  you

